## Proof

We work over $M_n(\mathbb{C})$. A **correlation matrix** is a positive semidefinite (PSD) matrix $B$ whose diagonal equals the identity, i.e. $B_{ii}=1$ for all $i$.

**Upper bound.** For any correlation matrix $B$, since $B$ is PSD, every eigenvalue $\lambda_i(B)\ge 0$. The trace equals the sum of eigenvalues, and
$$\operatorname{tr}(B)=\sum_{i=1}^n B_{ii}=n.$$
Hence $\sum_{i=1}^n \lambda_i(B)=n$. In particular
$$\lambda_{\max}(B)\le \sum_{i=1}^n \lambda_i(B)=n.$$
So $n$ is an upper bound for the set $\{\lambda_{\max}(B):B\text{ correlation matrix}\}$.

**The bound is attained.** Let $J\in M_n(\mathbb{C})$ be the all-ones matrix, $J_{ij}=1$ for all $i,j$. Then:
- $\operatorname{diag}(J)=(1,1,\dots,1)$, so its diagonal is $I$.
- $J=\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^*$ where $\mathbf{1}=(1,\dots,1)^*$, hence $J$ is PSD (it is a rank-one Gram matrix).
- The eigenvalues of $J$ are $n$ (with eigenvector $\mathbf{1}$) and $0$ with multiplicity $n-1$.

Thus $J$ is a correlation matrix and $\lambda_{\max}(J)=n$.

**Conclusion.** The maximum eigenvalue of every correlation matrix is at most $n$, and the value $n$ is realized by the all-ones matrix $J$. Therefore the supremum (in fact the maximum) of the set of maximum eigenvalues of $n\times n$ correlation matrices is

$$\boxed{n}.$$

